# The box

A page is a box inside a box. The outer one is the sheet, given here as an
explicit pair rather than a paper name, and the inner one is what is left after
the margins are taken off each edge.

## Four units, four edges

The four margins are written in four different units on purpose: millimetres,
points, and inches all have to arrive at the same measure, and an edge that
quietly fell back to a default would move every line on every page.

## What the running heads say

The header is off on the first page and on from the second, so the first page
of a document is not stamped like a continuation of something. Each slot is
interpolated once per page and drawn as an artifact, outside the structure
tree, because a running head is furniture rather than content.

# The second chapter

Page numbering restarts here, which is the whole point of naming a level: the
count is a property of the chapter, not of the sheet, and the footer has to
follow it. Roman numerals are spelled out from a table rather than asked of the
host locale, so this document numbers its pages the same way everywhere.

## A table of contents comes first

The contents are emitted ahead of the body and take a break after themselves,
so the body opens on a page of its own. Entries go two levels deep, which is
why the third-level heading below is absent from the list above.

### Not in the contents

Depth is a promise about the list, not about the document. This heading is
still a heading, still bookmarked, and still in the structure tree; it is only
missing from the page the reader was given to navigate by.

## The last of it

The final paragraph exists so that the second chapter is long enough to need a
page the first one did not, and so the total in the footer is a number that had
to be counted rather than guessed.
